  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 251 AM EDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nToday-Thursday... Upper level ridging across the Plains and the Deep \nSouth will stay in place through the time period. At the surface, \nhigh pressure across the Atlantic will strengthen and continue to \nbuild across the Florida peninsula and into the Gulf, with the ridge \naxis lifting northward. This will allow Tropical Storm (TS) Bertha \nto move westward along to just south of the northern Gulf Coast  \nthrough midweek. Locally, winds will weaken as the ridge axis \nmoves northward and the winds gradually become more onshore, with \nS/SE winds today and E/SE winds on Thursday. The east coast sea \nbreeze is forecast to form each day and move inland, which will \nturn the winds more onshore behind the sea breeze. \n\nDrier air will settle over the area, with forecast PW values ranging \nfrom 1.5-1.7\", which will result in reduced rain chances through the \nperiod. Lower than normal rain chances are forecast, with a low to \nmedium (20-40 percent) chance of rain and lightning storms today and \na low (20 percent) chance on Thursday. Highest coverage of storms \ntoday will be around the Lake Okeechobee area during the afternoon, \nand then focused west of Orlando with the sea breeze collision near \nto after sunset. Due to the more dominate onshore flow on Thursday, \nhighest coverage will be across the western interior in the \nafternoon/early evening. Some storms may be strong. Forecast \nsoundings support this with plenty of instability (MUCAPE 1500-2000 \nJ/kg), drier air aloft, Steep low-level lapse rates, and sufficient \ndowndraft potential (DCAPE  700-1200 J/kg). Because of this, the \nmain storm hazards will be frequent lightning strikes, and gusty \nwinds to 50 mph, and locally heavy rainfall. \n\nTemperatures will be near to above normal, with afternoon highs in \nthe low to mid 90s, with more widespread mid 90s across the \ninterior, each afternoon. Due to the drier air aloft, dewpoints \nshould be lower as mixing increases into the afternoon. This will \nallow peak heat indices to reach 100-107F, which remains below Heat \nAdvisory criteria. Warm and humid conditions will persist overnight, \nwith lows generally in the mid to upper 70s each night. \n\nFriday-Monday...  Upper level high pressure over the Plains and the \nDeep South will remain in place as a trough over the Midwest shifts \neastward towards the Atlantic coast and digs southward towards the \nFLorida into the weekend and early next week. At the surface, the \nAtlantic high pressure will shift southward through the weekend as a \nlow pressure system pushes off the coast of the Carolinas. An \nassociated frontal boundary will dig through the Deep South into \nlate week and into northern Florida before stalling over the \nweekend. The front will then drift slowly southward into early next \nweek as it weakens. There remains some uncertainty with how far \nsouth this boundary will go before it stalls. Locally, lingering dry \nair across the north on Friday will keep rain chances limited, with \nmoisture increasing across the south, causing a return of near to \nabove normal rain chances. Moisture will continue to increase across \nthe area this weekend, with shower and lightning storm activity \nprimarily being driven by sea breeze interactions/collisions. \nOffshore (westerly) flow will also return this weekend and continue \ninto the next week, which will favor higher coverage of storms \nacross the eastern side of the peninsula near the coast. Monday, \nrain and storm chances will be near to normal areawide as deep \nmoisture from the decaying boundary reaches the local area, with \nnear to above normal rain and storm chances on Tuesday as lingering \nmoisture is aided by normal sea breeze interactions/collisions. Any \nlingering showers and storms will diminish each evening through the \novernight hours. However, isolated to scattered showers will remain \npossible across the Atlantic waters each night.\n\nA slight warming trend is forecast through the weekend, with above \nnormal temperatures forecast through Sunday. Current guidance \nindicates that increasing cloud coverage and rain chances will help \nkeep temperatures closer to normal Monday and Tuesday. Peak heat \nindices will generally be 100-107F, with some locations potentially \nnearing Heat Advisory criteria, especially over the weekend. \nConfidence in this remains low however, so will continue to monitor. \nLows will generally be in the 70s each night.",
